{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773864697,"nonce":"L16MFSF6F2E8f93S5j2zFraJ4qU=","title":"Investigate subagent→parent result pruning via PostToolUse / MCP wrapping","message":"When a subagent (Agent tool) returns a large result to its parent, that full text enters the parent context verbatim. This is the highest-leverage point for context pruning — Mode B (LLM-mediated summarization) — because unlike built-in tool results (Read, Bash, etc.) where PostToolUse can only *add* additionalContext rather than replace, the Agent tool result may be interceptable.\n\nTwo paths to investigate:\n\n1. PostToolUse on the Agent tool: does the hook receive the subagent response text? Can additionalContext be used to inject a Haiku-generated summary while replacing (or at least outweighing) the verbose original? Or does the original still appear in full?\n\n2. MCP wrapping: could the Agent tool be surfaced as an MCP tool so that updatedMCPToolOutput can replace the result entirely? This would allow genuine replacement rather than additive injection.\n\nIf either path works, the implementation would be: PostToolUse fires on Agent tool completion → if result exceeds N tokens → Haiku call to extract/summarize relevant portion → inject summary in place of full result.\n\nEconomics: Haiku summarization call (~$0.003 for 10K-token input) vs Opus context savings (~$0.14 per 10K tokens saved). Ratio is favorable. Latency cost: ~200-500ms per large subagent result.\n\nAlso worth checking: whether Claude Code already compacts/truncates Agent tool results automatically the way it does for Bash/Read outputs.\n\nRelated discussion: opencode-style harnesses prune tool results before they enter parent context; for built-in tools this is not currently possible via hooks, but subagent results are the one place it might be.","files":null}]}